_to_delete/
_claude_scratch/
pet-district/
*.patch

# Delivery scripts and their payloads (see CLAUDE.md § Delivery).
#
# One per commit, each carrying a base64 copy of a patch that is ALREADY in
# this history — written to be run once on the Mac, not to be kept. `*.patch`
# above is the same class of thing and was ignored for the same reason; these
# were simply never added to the rule.
#
# The cost of not having this: `git status` at the repo root answers with
# eighty-odd `??` lines, which is enough noise that a landing script's own
# "is the tree clean" precondition read them as a dirty tree and refused to
# run. A status nobody can read is a check nobody performs.
#
# HONEST CAVEAT: 289 of these were committed before this rule and STAY
# tracked — .gitignore governs untracked files only. So the repo root is
# half-committed scripts, half-ignored ones until somebody finishes the job:
#     git rm -r --cached --ignore-unmatch 'land-*.sh' 'push-*.sh' 'resume-*.sh' 'amend-*.sh'
# That is a ~290-file deletion commit and it is deliberately NOT part of this
# change; the scripts are redundant with the commits they carry, but removing
# them is a decision, not a tidy-up.
/land-*.sh
/land-*.sh.b64
/push-*.sh
/resume-*.sh
/amend-*.sh

# Session artefacts written to the repo root — reports, audits, exported
# sheets and dashboards. They belong to the project docs; the copy here is a
# working file. Named one by one, ON PURPOSE: a pattern like `/*.md` would
# silently swallow the next document that DOES belong in the repo, and a
# .gitignore that hides work is worse than one that shows noise.
/Beauty-1M-Stress-Test-Second-Run-26-Aug.md
/Two-Rooms-One-Mira-21-Aug.md
/Two-Rooms-One-Mira-Landed-21-Aug.md
/beauty-audit-dashboard-26-aug.html
/beauty-brand-backlog.xlsx
/beauty-claims-to-fill.xlsx
/beauty-core-database.json
/beauty-core-database.xlsx
